  "notes": "⚠ **The only jazz-functional harmony in this batch, and the research flags it as unusual for the lane.** A George Benson sample provided the bed, so the source harmony is major-mode jazz-soul and the progressions are `Imaj7–vi7–ii7–V7`-family cycles. Those cycles are authored as `I–vi–ii–V` with the colour moved into `extensions` — 0.12 triad against 0.58 seventh and 0.30 ninth, by far the most coloured chord block here — because this engine's numerals carry no chord-quality suffix; the sevenths in the research's spelling are the extension weights, not the roman numerals. `1_per_bar` leads the harmonic rhythm 5:2 because a turnaround changes every bar. ⚠ The drums are the opposite of every other Battlecat-adjacent entry here: \"light, un-aggressive programming so the sung vocal sits forward\", which is `velocityTiers.accent` at 92–112 and `main` at 66–80 — the quietest kit in the batch — plus a laid-back swing at 0.6 with `snare.offGridMs` 8, the latest backbeat authored here. ⚠ The bass is the documented turning point of the record: an *added* bassline, live-feel, melodic, syncopated, the co-lead with the vocal — `followRootsProb` 0.4 with `anticipationProb` 0.36 and a major third in the passing tones, which no other entry in this batch has. Verses are sung rather than spoken, so the melody is chord-toned at 0.8 with `question_answer` phrasing and stays in a vocal 64–86; the instrumental countermelody stays in the low-mid at `registerOffset` −12, as the research specifies. ⚠ `confidence` is `medium` per the research's high-medium rating; **the inferred parameter is the melody register**, which follows from \"the vocal *is* the melody\" rather than from a stated range.",
